    {
      "slug": "reddit-hoa-wheelchair-ramp",
      "script": "An HOA fined what? Nobody wanted to be HOA secretary, so I took it. Then the board president fined a family four hundred dollars for an unapproved modification: a plywood wheelchair ramp for their new stepdad. I checked the bylaws. Federal fair-housing law makes disability modifications exempt—no board vote, no fine. I put that in writing and cc'd the whole board. The president called it a slippery slope. So I read three years of ignored complaints. Half targeted sheds and fences the president had personally approved for himself without a single board vote. That paperwork was selective. I filed every one, properly, with photos. The county fined the HOA for the unapproved additions, reversed the ramp fine, and the president resigned two meetings later—right after my fair-housing complaint got a callback. Was the wheelchair ramp really the slippery slope?",
      "editor_note": "Punched up to a four-word question hook, compressed the bylaws-versus-president escalation, added one dry paperwork aside away from the disability itself, and landed on the wheelchair-ramp slippery slope while preserving every fact and shot phrase."
    },
    {
      "slug": "reddit-airport-bag-sizer",
      "script": "Zero exceptions—for whom? Corporate's new rule: every carry-on gets the metal sizer or gets checked and charged. My supervisor drilled it into us: zero exceptions, three times. Then a man in a suit arrived, bag too big, and dropped his boss's name like it was a boarding pass. My supervisor started waving him through. I asked her to confirm the rule in front of him—she'd repeated it that morning. That policy needed a personality test. She couldn't contradict herself, so the bag went to the sizer, didn't fit, and got checked and charged like everyone else's. He missed his connection complaining to a manager for twenty minutes. Corporate pulled the boarding footage, saw her waving other bags through all week, and the exceptions stopped that day—for everyone, sizer and all. Was his boss's name too big for the sizer?",
      "editor_note": "Punched up to a three-word question hook, tightened the zero-exceptions reversal, added one dry policy aside, and landed on whether the boss's name fit the sizer while preserving every fact and shot phrase."
    }
  ],
